#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129976} :activity/id #uuid "9eb9ab54-1ec4-5368-a2e1-150d6811c312" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048584 :topic/id #uuid "abbdc596-7744-5a68-9999-a74874659d19" :topic/math-academy-id 2018} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129976 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130353 :tutorial/content "Rotation-scaling matrices are involved in an important theorem regarding complex eigenvalues.\n\nTo sketch out the key idea, let's consider the matrix \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-5 & -10 \\\\ 4 & 7\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhich has the complex eigenvalue \n\n$$\n\\lambda=1-2\\textrm{i}={\\color{blue}1}+({\\color{red}-2})\\textrm{i}\n$$\n\nand the corresponding complex eigenvector \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2-\\text{i} \\\\ -1+\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we construct a rotation-scaling matrix $C$ using the real and imaginary parts of the eigenvalue $\\lambda$ as follows:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWe also construct a matrix $P$ whose columns are the real and imaginary parts of the corresponding eigenvector:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe **rotation-scaling theorem** states that $A$ can be factored as follows:\n\n$$\nA=PCP^{-1}\n$$\n\n \n\nLet's check that this is true in this case:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}PC{P}^{-1} & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -5 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & -10 \\\\ 4 & 7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =A\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "d79a993f-cb2c-5ec9-a24b-ddc7bd94485d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3594 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "19750f8a-a131-5f4c-9488-2c2c664f3b38" :step/math-academy-id 20167 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129978}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129977 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129134 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128945 :question/id #uuid "1b13d9bb-9bcf-50be-a58d-39e82d99b9bf" :question/math-academy-id "e-5530"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b049dea4-5045-57ec-ad6a-c49f30e5da1f" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129662} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129666} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272111} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326740} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362361} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388737} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392266} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395616} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395623}] :knowledge-point/title "Converting a Matrix to Rotation-Scaling Form Given Multiple Parts of the Decomposition"} :step/id #uuid "e7048fe4-4391-5227-903b-216c1855d7e0" :step/math-academy-id 20168 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130109}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129978 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130354 :tutorial/content "Let's now formally state the rotation-scaling theorem:\n\n> *Let $A$ be a $2 \\times 2$ matrix with real entries. If $\\lambda$ is a complex (not real) eigenvalue of $A,$ and $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector corresponding to $\\lambda,$ then *\n> \n> \n> $$\n> A = PCP^{-1}\n> $$\n> \n> \n> *where*\n> \n> \n> $$\n> [\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]\n> $$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- When two matrices $A$ and $C$ are related via the equation $A = PCP^{-1},$ we say that they are **similar.**\n\n- The rotation-scaling theorem tells us that every $2 \\!\\times\\! 2$ real matrix with complex (non-real) eigenvalues is similar to a rotation-scaling matrix.\n\n- We call the right-hand side of $A = PCP^{-1}$ the **rotation-scaling form** of $A.$ We can also refer to this as the **rotation-scaling decomposition** of $A.$\n\n- The matrix $P$ *must* be constructed from the eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue defined by $C.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "09a26114-9068-50b4-897e-76504d4f7e68" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3595 :tutorial/title "The Rotation-Scaling Theorem"} :step/id #uuid "6e5f25d2-d5e2-5abe-a1c6-08572dd50606" :step/math-academy-id 20169 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129979}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129979 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129135 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128946 :question/id #uuid "26e641c9-bda5-5b93-8137-f4ec744ddb5c" :question/math-academy-id "e-5531"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1dcdd3f4-c03e-5887-a493-05598dbe4fc3" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049192}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129663} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129712} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326741} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362358} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362360} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402642}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors of a Matrix in Rotation-Scaling Form"} :step/id #uuid "b2e60b35-aad7-5404-bbeb-360484d2e855" :step/math-academy-id 20170 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129977}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129980 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129136 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128947 :question/id #uuid "75f39ec3-0963-5555-b418-d5b2df3be0ec" :question/math-academy-id "e-5532"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "148c97a4-24c1-5289-ad80-bee4af34509b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048973}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129664} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129665} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272113} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326692} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326742} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326743} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362371} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362373} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368528}] :knowledge-point/title "Converting a Matrix to Rotation-Scaling Form Given Only One Eigenvalue"} :step/id #uuid "380e2b24-5a1b-580a-9d3e-20419aa321c4" :step/math-academy-id 20171 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129981}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129981 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130355 :tutorial/content "Suppose $A$ is a real $2\\!\\times\\! 2$ matrix. To interpret the rotation-scaling form $A=PCP^{-1},$ we should bear in mind the following:\n\n- The matrix $C$ rotates vectors $\\mathbf x\\in\\mathbb R^2$ and then scales them.\n\n- We can think of $P$ as representing the following change of variable:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf x = P\\mathbf u \\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow\\qquad  \\mathbf u = P^{-1}\\mathbf x\n$$\n\nTherefore, $A$ can be thought of as a change of variable from $\\mathbf x$ to $\\mathbf u,$ followed by a rotation and scaling before returning to the original variable.\n\n![](images/ab/ab40869137296cea63fe209a842a4713fcae4fbf53c5089e6722b70bdf3f3230.png)\n\nFor example, let's consider the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3}+1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & \\sqrt{3}-1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis is a real matrix with complex eigenvalues and is thus similar to a rotation-scaling matrix. \n\nUsing the techniques described in this lesson, we can show that the rotation-scaling decomposition of $A$ is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe matrix $C$ can be written explicitly as a constant multiple of a rotation matrix as follows:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThus, the matrix $C$ represents the following:\n\n- a rotation of $\\arccos\\left(\\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{2}\\right) = \\dfrac\\pi 6$ counterclockwise about $O,$ and\n\n- an enlargement of scale factor ${\\color{blue}{2}}.$\n\nThe image of a unit square centered at the origin under the action of $A$ can therefore be decomposed as follows:\n\n![](images/92/92f2ab84b9ac8f36a93c42bf93b70b08a3e64eb3920a8bd2d5276217d936ab79.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "5e77afe5-c30a-56bd-95a6-037f1a903eda" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3596 :tutorial/title "The Geometric Interpretation of Rotation-Scaling Form"} :step/id #uuid "ed8aecde-9d1f-5061-afa2-aa5cc7c1036e" :step/math-academy-id 20172} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130109 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129214 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129033 :question/id #uuid "e0469305-b1d9-5430-9911-2f0c209b3264" :question/math-academy-id "e-8734"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "88f3ba21-6407-503e-aec0-fa4462a69e73" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048305}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129717} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129718} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362364} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362366} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362369} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367561} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388744} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395651} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395665}] :knowledge-point/title "Converting a Matrix to Rotation-Scaling Form Using the Complex Conjugate"} :step/id #uuid "7ce1fcbb-0bb6-5b74-ba7c-4006f8e5f481" :step/math-academy-id 32487 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129980}}] :activity/title "Reducing Real 2x2 Matrices to Rotation-Scaling Form" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186129361}]}
